  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'Analysis of water in a place shows that the water contains calcium chloride. People in that place are advised to use detergents for washing clothes. State at least two reasons. (3 marks)', 'Calcium chloride makes the water hard. (1) Soap (sodium stearate) reacts with Ca²⁺ ions to form insoluble calcium stearate (scum): 2C₁₇H₃₅COONa + CaCl₂ → (C₁₇H₃₅COO)₂Ca↓ + 2NaCl, so much soap is wasted, it does not lather and scum sticks to clothes. (2) Synthetic detergents (sodium alkylbenzene sulphonates) do not form insoluble salts with calcium or magnesium ions, so they lather and clean well even in hard water, leaving clothes clean without deposits.', null, '[{"point": "Water is hard (Ca²⁺); soap reacts with Ca²⁺ to form insoluble calcium salts (scum), wasting soap and not lathering", "marks": 1.5}, {"point": "Detergents (sulphonates) do not form insoluble precipitates with Ca²⁺/ Mg²⁺, so they lather and clean in hard water; also less residue on clothes", "marks": 1.5}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
