  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'Dry air was passed successively through a solution of 5 g of a solute in 180 g of water and then through pure water. The loss in weight of the solution was 2.50 g and that of the pure solvent was 0.04 g. What is the molecular weight of the solute? (3 marks)', 'By the Ostwald-Walker method, the loss in weight of the solution is proportional to p (vapour pressure of solution) and the loss of the pure solvent to (p° − p). So (p° − p)/p° = 0.04/(2.50 + 0.04) = 0.04/2.54 = 0.0157. Relative lowering of vapour pressure = mole fraction of solute ≈ n₂/n₁ = (5/M)/(180/18) = 0.5/M. Hence M = 0.5/0.0157 ≈ 31.8 g/mol (using (p° − p)/p = n₂/n₁ gives 0.5/0.016 = 31.25). The molecular weight is about 32.', null, '[{"point": "Ostwald-Walker: (p° − p)/p° = loss of solvent/(total loss) = 0.04/2.54 (or (p° − p)/p = 0.04/2.50)", "marks": 1.5}, {"point": "= n₂/n₁ ≈ (5/M)/(180/18) → M = 0.5/0.0157 ≈ 31.8 g/mol (≈ 31-32)", "marks": 1.5}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
